# =============================================================================
# AddressSanitizer suppression file for gRPC / Abseil false positives
#
# This file is a safety net for builds configured with SANITIZE_DEPS=OFF, where
# gRPC, Abseil and protobuf come from the toolchain as *prebuilt, uninstrumented*
# libraries. That mix produces false positives at the library boundary: the
# instrumented copies of inline C++ symbols (std::string_view,
# std::char_traits, ...) are weak, so the dynamic linker interposes them into
# the uninstrumented gRPC dylibs. Those instrumented inlines then run on stack
# frames built by uninstrumented code, where ASan has no frame descriptor, and
# it misattributes leftover shadow poison to whatever frame is nearest.
#
# The reports are recognisable by exactly that misattribution -- e.g. an access
# reported at "offset 544" inside a frame whose objects only span 104 bytes,
# with ASan's own "this may be a false positive" hint.
#
# With the default SANITIZE_DEPS=ON the dependencies are rebuilt from source with
# -fsanitize=address, so the sanitizer gets a whole-program view and none of these
# suppressions are needed -- the full suite passes without them. The same holds
# under Bazel, where --config=asan reaches every C++ target in the graph.
# See doc/build.md.
#
# Usage:
#   ASAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=scripts/asan.supp ctest --preset asan
#
# The `asan` test preset in CMakePresets.json applies this automatically.
#
# NOTE: ASan's suppression mechanism only understands interceptor_via_fun,
# interceptor_via_lib and odr_violation -- it can only silence errors reported
# from inside an interceptor (memcmp, strlen, ...). Errors raised by a direct
# instrumented load/store check cannot be suppressed by this file at all, which
# is why SANITIZE_DEPS=ON is the default rather than a longer suppression list.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Errors reported from ASan's own interceptors (memcmp, strcmp, strlen, ...)
# when the calling frame belongs to an uninstrumented third-party library.
#
# Observed instance:
#   use-after-poison in MemcmpInterceptorCommon
#     <- std::char_traits<char>::compare
#     <- absl::StartsWith                       (match.h)
#     <- grpc_core::URI::ToString               (uri.cc)
#     <- grpc_sockaddr_to_uri                   (sockaddr_utils.cc)
#     <- grpc_core::Subchannel::SetConnectivityStateLocked
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
interceptor_via_lib:libgrpc
interceptor_via_lib:libgrpc++
interceptor_via_lib:libabsl
interceptor_via_lib:libprotobuf
